Mortar stays out of routing. Your routes decide which document to show, read it, and render it inside your own layout. Mortar renders the body.

## Provide the content

Wrap your app in `ContentContext` with a `ContentSource`: two functions that list and get documents, usually your server functions.

```tsx
import { ContentContext } from '@mortar-kit/core';

<ContentContext value={{ list: listDocs, get: getDoc }}>
  <Router>...</Router>
</ContentContext>
```

## Read a document

`useContent()` gives a typed reader: pass a collection object and you get documents with typed fields.

```tsx
import { PageView, useContent } from '@mortar-kit/core';
import { useParams } from '@solidjs/router';
import { createMemo, Show } from 'solid-js';

export default function PostRoute() {
  const params = useParams<{ slug: string }>();
  const content = useContent();
  const post = createMemo(() => content.get(posts, { slug: params.slug }));
  return (
    <Show when={post()} fallback={<NotFound />}>
      {(p) => (
        <article>
          <h1>{p().fields.title}</h1>
          <PageView bricks={bricks} entries={p().entries ?? []} />
        </article>
      )}
    </Show>
  );
}
```

Reads return promises. A memo holding a promise is pending until it lands, so put a `<Loading>` boundary around your routes. On the server the page waits for it, so the HTML holds the content.

Lists work the same way:

```ts
const latest = createMemo(() => content.list(posts, { limit: 5 }));
const inCategory = createMemo(() => content.list(products, { where: { category: categoryId() } }));
```

`where` keeps documents whose `id`, `slug` or top-level field equals each value. `sort` overrides the collection's order, and `limit` takes 1 to 100. Lists leave out bodies.

## Layouts

Detail pages are your code: a post's title and date, a product's price. Keep them in layout components, so the admin's preview can render documents inside the same layouts (see [The admin](/docs/admin)).

## Missing documents

`get` returns `null` when there's no document. Render your own not-found view, and set the status with `httpStatus(404)` from `@solidjs/web` when rendering on the server.
